# _redirects - Cloudflare Pages legacy-URL map for trafficchallan.com
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# This domain was drop-caught. Before we bought it, the same hostname ran a
# WordPress blog. This file forwards the handful of that blog's URLs whose
# subject matter a page on this site actually covers, so a stray old link
# lands on something useful instead of a 404. Nothing else.
#
# HOW MUCH IS AT STAKE: ESSENTIALLY NOTHING. MEASURED, NOT GUESSED.
# Do not over-invest in this file. The recoverable value is about zero:
#   * Wayback Machine CDX index for the host, queried 2026-08-19
#     (http://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=trafficchallan.com&matchType=domain):
#     across the entire archive there are 127 captures and NOT ONE capture of
#     any inner page of the apex host. Only "/", "/robots.txt", "/favicon.ico"
#     and "/about-us" were ever crawled - and "/about-us" returned 404 on all
#     nine of its captures (2014-10-05 ... 2016-11-29), i.e. it never existed.
#   * A Common Crawl index check across 17 crawls spanning 2018-2026 returned
#     zero captures of this host, including while the blog was live and
#     publishing. (Measured before this file was written; the Common Crawl
#     index API was answering 504 on 2026-08-19 and this was not re-run.)
#   * Bing shows zero indexed pages for the host.
# If crawlers never fetched those URLs, essentially nobody links to them. This
# file exists because it costs a few minutes and catches any stray link, NOT
# because there is link equity waiting to be reclaimed. Nothing in this repo
# should ever claim otherwise.
#
# WHERE THE OLD URL LIST CAME FROM
# The inner pages themselves are unarchived, so the list was reconstructed by
# parsing every internal <a href> out of the three archived apex homepages
# that returned 200 with the blog on them:
#   https://web.archive.org/web/20240726015355id_/https://trafficchallan.com/
#   https://web.archive.org/web/20240923220128id_/https://trafficchallan.com/
#   https://web.archive.org/web/20250331121826id_/https://trafficchallan.com/
# (fetch those id_ URLs with `curl --compressed`; without it you get gzip bytes
# that look like an empty page). That yields 184 distinct old paths: 141 /tag/
# archives, 26 dated /YYYY/MM/DD/slug/ posts, and the WordPress furniture -
# author archives, /category/, /page/N/, /sample-page/, feeds, /wp-json/,
# /xmlrpc.php. The 2025-03-31 capture is a second, later WordPress install
# using /index.php/ permalinks; its URLs are in here too.
#
# THE RULE FOR ADDING ANYTHING HERE
# Google's site-move guidance, verbatim (read 2026-08-19,
# https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/site-move-with-url-changes):
#   "Don't redirect many old URLs to one irrelevant single URL destination,
#    such as the home page of the new site. This can confuse users and might
#    be treated as a soft 404 error."
# So: an old path is redirected ONLY when a live page here covers the same
# subject. Where there is no good match it is left to 404 - a 404 is the
# honest answer and is safer than a topic-flip redirect.
# 43 of the 184 recovered paths qualified. 141 did not, and are deliberately
# absent:
#   * About two thirds of the old blog was exam results, government-job
#     recruitment and celebrity-net-worth churn (UGC NET, KSET, DSSSB, AP TET,
#     IIT Dhanbad recruitment, "sarkari result", Munawar Faruqui income, Tata
#     Technologies IPO ...). Pointing those at challan pages is precisely the
#     expired-domain topic flip Google's spam policy targets. They 404.
#   * Toll-plaza and NHAI tags, rights / police-jurisdiction tags, Gujarati
#     news tags, and one-word autotags (/tag/bike/, /tag/car/, /tag/road/,
#     /tag/india/) have no page here that answers them. They 404.
#   * Author archives, /page/N/, /sample-page/, feeds, /wp-json/ and
#     /xmlrpc.php are WordPress furniture, not content. They 404. Never
#     recreate them: that content is not ours, and rebuilding a previous
#     owner's pages to catch residual links is expired-domain abuse.
#
# SYNTAX AND LIMITS - confirmed 2026-08-19 against Cloudflare's own docs,
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#   * one rule per line: "[source] [destination] [code?]"; 302 is the default
#     status code, so every line here states 301 explicitly
#   * "Lines starting with a # will be treated as comments."
#   * 2,000 static + 100 dynamic redirects, 2,100 combined. We ship 47 lines,
#     all static - 2.4% of the static budget.
#   * "Each redirect declaration has a 1,000-character limit." The longest
#     line here is 225 characters.
#   * query strings CANNOT be matched in the source (documented unsupported),
#     which is why nothing here tries to match ?p=123 style URLs
#   * "Redirects are always followed, regardless of whether or not an asset
#     matches the incoming request." A rule here would therefore SHADOW a live
#     page at the same path - tests/redirects.test.ts fails if any source
#     collides with a path this site builds.
#   * "If there are multiple redirects for the same source path, the top-most
#     redirect is applied." Duplicate sources are also a test failure.
# No splats (*) or placeholders (:name) are used anywhere in this file. A
# pattern rule cannot know whether the path it swallows is on-topic, and this
# whole file is an exercise in only redirecting what we have checked one URL
# at a time.
#
# EVERY DESTINATION IS VERIFIED TO EXIST. tests/redirects.test.ts parses this
# file and asserts each destination is a page this repo actually builds; a
# redirect to a 404 is worse than no redirect. If you delete or rename a page,
# that test tells you which line here to fix.
#
# The percent-encoded Gujarati sources are listed twice, in the lower-case hex
# the old WordPress install emitted in its own links and in the upper-case hex
# RFC 3986 normalises to. Whether Cloudflare's matcher case-folds percent
# escapes is not documented; two lines is cheaper than finding out in
# production.

